  "verdict": "Missing context",
  "steelman": "Peer-reviewed ecological models and federal wildlife agency reviews estimate that collisions with land-based wind turbines in the contiguous United States kill between 140,000 and 328,000 birds each year, putting the annual direct mortality squarely in the hundreds of thousands.",
  "finding": "Estimates suggest that between 140,000 and 328,000 birds are killed annually by collisions with turbines at wind energy facilities in the contiguous United States. However, this raw figure provides a total without a rate, omitting that wind turbines account for less than 1% of the overall estimated annual avian fatality documented in the United States.",
  "reasoning_flags": [
    "Totals, not rates"
  ],
  "newest_data": "Relied on comprehensive U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service assessments and the foundational Loss et al. collision mortality syntheses reviewed in The Condor (American Ornithological Society). Searches of federal agency repositories and ornithological literature through October 2026 confirm that these syntheses remain the standard benchmark figures utilized by the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, with no newer comprehensive national census superseding them.",
